dr anthony fauci has served under seven  presidents  and led america's response to hiv aids  ebola and zika  but during the kovid 19 pandemic  he found himself at odds with his  president battling misinformation  and targeted by personal attacks  did you hear that tony they want you  locked up  is this a passing phase  or is there something broken about  science and politics in america  [Music]  is it good enough for scientists to say  i'm just a political you know i can  stand aside from the science well we  should be a political and that's what we  and i certainly have tried to be but  unfortunately  when you get  um  totally politically motivated  inaccuracies  get thrown into the picture like the  claims that the interventions worked  when there was no indication  whatsoever that they work none at all  and it was taken as  a one person's word is as good as  another well when you're dealing with  science  one person's word is not as good as  another  the science determines what is the  correct  answer to a particular question you  yourself became a politicized figure so  as you were became a hero to someone and  a villain to others  how did you  attempt to hold that position of  neutrality what was your your strategy  my strategy is to always stick with the  science  be flexible enough that when new  information  evolves  in an evolving outbreak  to make sure that you're flexible enough  to modify your position on public health  issues  um i've done that  to the best of my ability but  the political issues that have come in  you get accused of flip-flopping now  that's very interesting because if  scientists  did not change  their approach to things as new data  evolved they would be violating the  principles of science  and yet that has been interpreted as  flip-flopping the other situation  that i think triggered it  for me  is that when i was part of the  white house coronavirus task force  in the trump administration i was put in  the very uncomfortable position to  actually have to contradict the  president of the united states  who is a very politically polarized  president as you know at the time  and  it was something i was really quite  uncomfortable in doing i took no  pleasure at all i have a phenomenal  respect  for the institutions of my country  including the presidency  and the various  forms of government the legislative and  judicial branch  and the idea that i had to  at great discomfort  come out and essentially contradict and  correct the president that created  a  sort of a groundswell of  antithesis towards me or antipathy  that's the right word towards me on the  part  of the far right who were very much  you know aligned with trump um and all  of a sudden  i became the opposition  which was really unfortunate because i  was really being guided  purely by the science has it changed  your view of of public service i i mean  if if you were talking to a young  scientist now  would you say  look just stay in the lab and to do the  research um you know you could you can  do an awful lot of good writing papers  and researching into diseases  or would you still say look public  service is the thing to do  i would be very upset  if qualified young people  refrain from entering into public  service  because if they see what what i and some  of my scientific colleagues  are going through it is really  extraordinary you are probably aware  that when you have disagreements  disagreements are healthy  particularly when you discuss the basis  of the disagreement but now when you  have  scientists like myself and i'm i'm not  alone in this  who come out with a  science-based opinion they wind up  getting death threats  that's very common it's not it's not a  rarity it's it's quite common  that is  very disturbing and i really do hope  that we see our way  out of that  because that i think is going to  interfere  with the normal and appropriate  discourse and agreements and  disagreements and discussions when if  you give an opinion that disagrees with  somebody  you get threatened your family get  threatened that's really very bad news  i think we are living  right now  in a  society  both domestic here in the united states  and in some respects  globally  of  rather significant divisiveness  where something  like a public health issue  becomes  politicized and is influenced  by political ideology i mean we had  disagreements  when we had  hiv and  political differences with ebola and  political differences when we're dealing  with zika and all that  but never was it such  that there was such a profound  difference  in which in many respects  things that were completely untrue  are accepted by large numbers of people  and you have these  differences of opinion of things that  should not be any differences because  they're just facts  i'm sachin outer executive editor at the  economist  this was part of a digital event  produced exclusively for subscribers to  the economist if you'd like to watch the  full recording of this or any of our  other events please click the link  you'll have to register first or you can  subscribe to the economist and enjoy all  of our journalism thanks for watching
 

